00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.100
MARK MANDEL: So I know we've
had a really big push internally

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.360
around IAM and access control.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:35.140
I know there were some new
authorization facilities that

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:38.750
were in place with Kubernetes.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:41.010
Has that been extended
to IAM now as well?

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:41.884
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:07:41.884 --> 00:07:43.880
So they added role-based
access, or RBAC.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.990
And now with IAM
and GKE, what you

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:47.940
get is all the
API calls now, you

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.850
can say, OK, who has
access to do this?

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.330
Who can start up a new pod?

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.460
Who can delete stuff?

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.460
So that's support in GKE now.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:59.542
What IAM is is identity
and access management.

00:07:59.542 --> 00:08:01.000
MARK MANDEL: So
that's really cool.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.000
I mean, when you're running
a really large team,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.916
especially if you have
a really large cluster,

00:08:04.916 --> 00:08:06.800
you want to have control
over who can do what

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.169
within that cluster.

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:09.960
I think that sounds
really, really powerful

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:10.620
and really useful.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.494
CARTER MORGAN: So the
coolest thing about IAM

00:08:12.494 --> 00:08:16.070
is that instead of role-based
stuff where you say,

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:18.070
