you could have clusters
in different regions,

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:35.704
like Asia, Europe, US.

00:09:35.704 --> 00:09:38.120
You could also have them across
different cloud providers,

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:41.372
like AWS, GCP, or even
on prem, like OpenStack.

00:09:41.372 --> 00:09:43.080
Here what we're going
to get is something

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.650
replicated across
zones, so if you're

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.970
worried that a zone might fail.

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:47.930
It's lightweight.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:51.340
It's not the same as
a cluster federation.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:53.660
But what you can also do
is have different types

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.180
of nodes in your cluster.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:57.380
So I could say, maybe
I have a database

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.150
and I want a 200-gigabyte
machine for it

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:01.190
to sit on so it could
eat up that data.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.820
But my front-end
application or my server app

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.550
only needs like a 10-gig,
pretty small instance.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:07.580
So now I can do that.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:08.820
That's very powerful.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.230
MARK MANDEL: And I take it
you've got control there

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:12.310
as well in terms of
where your pods end up

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.062
sitting within that node pool.

00:10:14.062 --> 00:10:15.770
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah,
so there's affinity.

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:17.850
And you can also use selectors.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.690
So you could say, basically,
when you use node pools,

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:22.400
you get a label
attached to the node.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:23.480
It's an actual machine.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:25.230
And you can use that
in your pod to select

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:26.630
which one it's going to sit on.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:28.379
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that
sounds super useful,

00:10:28.379 --> 00:10:31.482
being able to
specifically say, hey,

00:10:31.482 --> 00:10:33.440
this container should be
on this type of thing.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.530
Now, to play devil's
advocate a little bit,

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:38.820
does that go against the
idea of cattle versus pets?

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.652
Or is that still in the
same cattle category?

00:10:41.652 --> 00:10:43.860
CARTER MORGAN: I think when
you have persistent data,

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:47.600
you no longer have cattle,
because data matters.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.860
Data isn't stateless.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:52.920
So maybe the whole
idea of persistent data

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.184
goes against cattle.

00:10:55.184 --> 00:10:55.850
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:58.050
But still something to consider
when dealing with node pools

00:10:58.050 --> 00:10:59.430
and how you structure stuff.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:00.810
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:01.280
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:02.490
There are pros and cons.

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:04.198
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
I wonder if having

00:11:04.198 --> 00:11:07.270
node pools plus having
local SSTs is something that

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:10.010
can make it like, you can
say all my web servers

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:13.950
are going to be running
on the light nodes.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.710
But then I have the
nodes with local SST

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:17.960
that are going to be
running whatever, [? ATD ?]

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.160
or whatever database
you want to use.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:21.630
Sounds like a good
thing to have.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.180
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, that
sounds like a perfect use case.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:24.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, nice.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.780
Something else related also
to nodes, not node pools.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:30.339
It is node auto scaling.

00:11:30.339 --> 00:11:31.130
CARTER MORGAN: Yes.

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:33.255
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I've seen
these many times before,

00:11:33.255 --> 00:11:35.760
and it was like you need
to make it-- you need

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.910
to write your own auto scaler.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:38.990
It was quite hard.

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:41.140
Seems like we fixed
that, and we have it now

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:43.860
as a part of
Kubernetes-- part of GKE.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.090
CARTER MORGAN:
GKE, yeah, and GCE.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:48.720
So basically now, there was
already horizontal pod auto

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.659
scaling, so if you used up
too many resources on pods,

00:11:52.659 --> 00:11:53.700
you could scale up a pod.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:55.030
That was built into Kubernetes.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:56.904
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
is-- for our listeners,

00:11:56.904 --> 00:11:59.647
what is horizontal scaling
versus vertical scaling?

00:11:59.647 --> 00:12:01.480
CARTER MORGAN: Good
question, good question.

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:02.970
So horizontal
scaling is scaling up

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:05.000
more instances of
your application,

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.850
whereas vertical scaling is
scaling up more resources

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:09.560
for your instances to live on.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:13.540
So now in GKE, what you get
is a vertical auto scaling.

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:15.636
So we can say, I
have this pod that I

00:12:15.636 --> 00:12:17.510
can't schedule right
now, because there's not

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:18.520
enough space for it.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:20.640
GKE's smart enough, our
computer's smart enough

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:23.180
to go and look and say, if
I were to add another node,

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:25.980
could I schedule this pod?

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.404
And if so, then schedule it.

00:12:27.404 --> 00:12:28.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:29.880
CARTER MORGAN: And
so there's a few use

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:31.879
cases for this, actually,
if you think about it.

00:12:31.879 --> 00:12:35.165
Take a CI service,
continuous integration.

00:12:35.165 --> 00:12:37.290
It's going to be really
busy Monday through Friday,

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.081
if you're not one of
those companies that's

00:12:39.081 --> 00:12:40.550
making people work all weekend.

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:42.800
And it's going to wind
down on the weekend.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:45.240
So why waste money having
all these resources up

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:46.100
over the weekend?

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:47.016
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:12:47.016 --> 00:12:49.364
CARTER MORGAN: You can
have your nodes scale up

00:12:49.364 --> 00:12:50.780
while everyone's
in there working,

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:53.190
and then over the weekend wind
back down and save you money.

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.356
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that
sounds like a very, very

00:12:55.356 --> 00:12:56.650
good use of your money.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:58.820
I'm wondering,
how does this work

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:01.280
with, again, stateful apps?

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:02.220
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:03.539
Yeah, I-- hm.

00:13:03.539 --> 00:13:05.330
I know there are some
things that are still

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:07.880
getting ironed out, like we
just talked about node affinity

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:08.881
or using node selectors.

00:13:08.881 --> 00:13:09.796
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:09.796 --> 00:13:12.210
CARTER MORGAN: Well, so if I
add a label, or a few other

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.120
[? QTTL ?] commands
to a node, that's

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:18.300
not going to get replicated
when I scale up a pod or not.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:22.043
So if you're using that
to say, I want my node

00:13:22.043 --> 00:13:24.000
to sit on this pod
because it's SST

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.930
and I want it to go fast in
this stateful reason, that's

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:28.050
not going to work.

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:29.925
MARK MANDEL: I suppose
that makes sense, too,

00:13:29.925 --> 00:13:33.750
like if you're auto scaling,
at least right now, especially,

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:35.320
if you're auto
scaling out a cluster,

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:37.800
it's for mostly stateless apps.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:41.080
I mean, that's the stuff you
really want to grow and shrink.

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:43.835
It's not for stuff that's
like-- growing and shrinking

00:13:43.835 --> 00:13:46.500
a database in real time
makes me really scared.

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:47.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:49.530
CARTER MORGAN: Are there people
that grow and shrink databases?

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:50.610
MARK MANDEL: I don't know.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.651
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I mean,
I'm pretty sure you can.

00:13:52.651 --> 00:13:56.170
Like I know that for Bigtable,
you can totally do that.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.200
It is not auto scaling, though.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.790
But you need to
be quite careful,

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:01.290
because if you
have-- like your data

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.160
is replicated on
three nodes and it

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:05.100
needs to be replicated
on three nodes,

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:08.240
and you turn down all
of those three nodes

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:10.430
that happen to have
one single key,

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:12.560
then you lose
data, which is bad.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.035
So I'm sure you can do it, but
you need to be really careful,

00:14:16.035 --> 00:14:16.535
I guess.

00:14:16.535 --> 00:14:17.300
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
it's the sort of thing

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:19.720
that I'd be like OK, I'm happy
doing this in a manual way,

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:21.690
but I don't know if I
want a machine doing it,

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:23.130
at least not yet.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:24.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:28.720
So what are your-- like, what
is your advice for people that

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:32.600
would like to run, maybe,
let's say something

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.570
typical like WordPress,
and you have the front end

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:37.295
and then you have the database.

00:14:37.295 --> 00:14:39.590
Where could you
put the database?

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:43.280
I could personally avoid
putting in containers.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:45.610
But I don't use
containers all the time.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:47.230
What is your opinion on that?

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.130
CARTER MORGAN: I think if I
could host a database, I would.

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:53.580
This is my most candid opinion.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:56.260
As in, I'd use a MySQL
service or something

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:59.800
like that that's hosted instead
of putting it in a container.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.539
But with the new
features in Kubernetes,

00:15:01.539 --> 00:15:03.080
I think we're going
to see a lot more

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.650
experimentation, a lot more
people trying it out, so maybe

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:07.427
new best practices
are going to emerge.

00:15:07.427 --> 00:15:08.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:10.650
MARK MANDEL: So maybe the
tooling is not quite there yet,

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:12.982
but as people start
to play with it,

00:15:12.982 --> 00:15:14.440
it'll get there at
a certain point.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:15.315
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:15:15.315 --> 00:15:17.660
We talked about the
1,000-node Cassandra demo.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.659
And again, it's a demo, so we
didn't see it in production.

00:15:20.659 --> 00:15:22.200
We didn't see how
long it would last.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.260
But that's really
exciting to me,

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:26.010
because if you can
scale up and scale down

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:27.384
like that with
Kubernetes, that's

00:15:27.384 --> 00:15:29.640
almost like using
a hosted service.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.416
So I don't know if
it's a release away

00:15:31.416 --> 00:15:32.540
or if it's ready right now.

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:34.970
I want to go play after
this and check it out.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:36.470
MARK MANDEL: Actually
combining that

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:40.000
with stuff like-- what's the
package manager for Kubernetes

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:40.750
called these days?

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:41.240
CARTER MORGAN: Helm.

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:41.680
MARK MANDEL: Is it Helm?

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:43.949
It's changed names about
three or four times, I think.

00:15:43.949 --> 00:15:45.490
But being able to
sit around and say,

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:47.670
hey, I want Cassandra, boom.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:50.405
I want a MongoDB
replica set-- boom.

00:15:50.405 --> 00:15:52.280
The combination of that
and those two things,

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:54.386
I think that is quite powerful.

00:15:54.386 --> 00:15:55.260
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:56.870
And so, again,
Kubernetes is supposed

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:59.360
to be a pluggable
framework like that.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:01.790
So I could see that
happening in the future.

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:04.340
It's an exciting time to
be working on Kubernetes.

00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:05.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:09.690
So there's a lot of really
fancy new things going around.

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:13.300
But I feel like there's
also, many of our listeners

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:18.600
are still are curious about, OK,
so I've never used Kubernetes.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.059
Where do I get started?

00:16:20.059 --> 00:16:21.850
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah,
I'm seeing that a lot,

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:23.670
especially going to
conferences and giving talks.

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:24.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:26.603
CARTER MORGAN: There's always
a big crowd of people that

00:16:26.603 --> 00:16:28.360
are like, we want to know more.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:31.410
To that end, Kelsey and I made
a course, Udacity scalable

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:33.380
microservices in Kubernetes.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:36.114
It goes over everything from,
OK, I have an application.

00:16:36.114 --> 00:16:37.530
How do I package
it and distribute

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:39.010
that in a doc or image?

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:41.280
What are some best
practices for that?

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.590
So we talk about
things like 12 Factor,

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.770
the microservices pattern,
which is the most overused term

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:50.220
in all of tech, containers.

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:52.900
We talk about the
basics of Kubernetes,

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:56.920
like deployment, services,
health and monitoring, secrets

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:57.496
and all that.

00:16:57.496 --> 00:16:58.870
And then we get
a little bit more

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:00.940
advanced and talk about
deployment patterns

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:02.930
and rolling updates
and things like that.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:05.400
So that would be
my first choice.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.177
I'm a little biased
there, you know.

00:17:07.177 --> 00:17:08.510
MARK MANDEL: That's fair enough.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:09.329
CARTER MORGAN:
We've got interviews

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:12.050
with really intelligent
people like Adrian Cockroft,

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:13.950
former cloud
architect at Netflix,

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:15.740
and he talks about
the pet versus cattle

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:17.940
divide a little bit as
well, and the importance

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:20.650
of infrastructure and
why you would use it,

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:23.139
when to write your own,
if ever, things like that.

00:17:23.139 --> 00:17:24.680
MARK MANDEL: That
sounds really cool.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:27.240
Does it cover any of the
cluster setup side of things?

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:27.990
CARTER MORGAN: No.

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:28.725
So we--

00:17:28.725 --> 00:17:30.600
MARK MANDEL: It's for
application developers.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:31.475
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:17:31.475 --> 00:17:34.670
And so that's one of
the things I'm learning.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:37.550
In this role-- I'm
a DPE, so my role

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:39.180
is [? being ?] customer zero.

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:41.660
And so I've got to start using
GKE and things like that.

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:44.214
I've got to start doing
Kubernetes the hard way.

00:17:44.214 --> 00:17:46.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it
makes it too easy for you.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:46.860
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:49.068
So in this course, though,
we wanted to make it easy.

00:17:49.068 --> 00:17:51.339
So what we did is we used
Container Engine, which

00:17:51.339 --> 00:17:52.880
is the easiest way
to use Kubernetes.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:57.170
It's pretty much one command
line, you're ready to go.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:01.300
And we also use Cloud Shell,
which is-- what is it?

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:04.150
It's a provision VM for you
that keeps your dot files.

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:07.310
You have a local space
on a disk somewhere.

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:09.600
It's a standard
operating environment.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:12.310
So it's probably the easiest way
to get started with Kubernetes,

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:15.016
is Cloud Shell and
Google Container Engine.

00:18:15.016 --> 00:18:17.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that
way everything you need

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:19.560
is just having a browser.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:20.203
That's it.

00:18:20.203 --> 00:18:20.626
CARTER MORGAN: Uh-huh.

00:18:20.626 --> 00:18:21.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:22.680
CARTER MORGAN: So it was a
pretty nice, pretty clean

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:23.490
setup.

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:25.635
We did miss-- there's a
few things in the course

00:18:25.635 --> 00:18:29.200
that if we were doing a
Kubernetes 201 we would do,

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:33.470
like more production use
cases, stateful applications,

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:37.320
name spaces, automatic
integration with the CI tool,

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:40.099
continuous integration,
continuous deployment.

00:18:40.099 --> 00:18:41.640
But yeah, if you
want to get started,

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:44.050
that course is the easiest,
quickest way to do it.

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:46.060
It's a free course--
Udacity in Google.

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:48.185
MARK MANDEL: Is that a
teaser, that you're actually

00:18:48.185 --> 00:18:50.780
going to do a 201, like
an advanced course?

00:18:50.780 --> 00:18:51.420
Is that a hint?

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:52.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Because
we're now excited about it.

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:53.560
CARTER MORGAN: You're
excited about it?

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:53.791
Uh-oh.

00:18:53.791 --> 00:18:54.484
