sure to pass them on to Carter.

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:20.060
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:21.120
100%.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:22.036
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:22.036 --> 00:19:25.396
So we're kind of running out
of time for this episode.

00:19:25.396 --> 00:19:26.770
Is there anything
else that you'd

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:28.980
like to mention about
Kubernetes or GKE

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:33.310
that we have not mentioned this
episode or the previous one?

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.440
CARTER MORGAN: I
guess just to wrap up,

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:36.830
Kubernetes makes these claims.

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:40.600
It says, plan at scale and
never outgrow and run anywhere.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:42.140
And with 1.3, we're
closer to that

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:43.900
than ever, because
you can run anywhere

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:48.240
with cluster federation, planet
scale, 2,000 nodes, 60,000

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:50.960
containers, and
never outgrow-- you

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:55.880
can go from local to
basically, again, 2,000 nodes.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.080
So Kubernetes is
something that you

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:00.850
can get started with now, trying
it out really easy with GKE

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:04.632
and Cloud Shell, and
then take it on prem

00:20:04.632 --> 00:20:07.090
or whatever you really need to
do to meet production needs.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:08.019
It's there for you.

00:20:08.019 --> 00:20:08.810
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:10.540
Well, thanks so much
