00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:25.300
We're going to go through
the question of the week.

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:26.010
MARK MANDEL: Yeah

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.710
CARTER MORGAN: And surprisingly,
it is also about Kubernetes.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:29.830
MARK MANDEL: Is it really?

00:20:29.830 --> 00:20:30.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:30.910
Shocking, right?

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:32.410
MARK MANDEL: I know.

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:35.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
so I think from you.

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:36.279
Why don't you ask it?

00:20:36.279 --> 00:20:37.820
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
this is a question

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:41.460
I've heard a lot from a bunch
of people using Kubernetes.

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:44.070
It's really around, how
do I do local development

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:45.634
with Kubernetes?

00:20:45.634 --> 00:20:47.300
We talked a little
bit about the podcast

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:48.330
as well, like
setting up a cluster

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:49.460
is a little bit difficult.

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:49.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:51.220
MARK MANDEL: There seems to have
been some options out there,

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:52.970
but there's a few of them.

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:53.910
Which one do I choose?

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:54.725
What do I do?

00:20:54.725 --> 00:20:56.850
So if I want to do local
development in Kubernetes,

00:20:56.850 --> 00:20:59.672
how do I do it, Carter?

00:20:59.672 --> 00:21:01.720
CARTER MORGAN: OK,
that's a great place.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:03.620
Minikube is the way to go.

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:05.530
So what Minikube
does is it sets you

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:08.900
up a local one-node
cluster using a VM.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.470
So by default, it's
virtual biased,

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:13.016
but you can specify
different ones.

00:21:13.016 --> 00:21:15.140
And then you have a lot of
the power of Kubernetes.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:16.270
You don't get everything.

00:21:16.270 --> 00:21:17.850
You don't get
ingress controllers.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.240
You don't get load balancers.

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:23.780
But for most of your day-to-day
use on a single-node machine,

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:26.690
you can try it out, and then
you can take that and just

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:29.770
go and run the same thing
that you're running locally

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:30.830
on a provider.

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:33.020
MARK MANDEL: So I know in
previous local versions

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:36.572
of Kubernetes, I was limited by
the number of pods I could run,

00:21:36.572 --> 00:21:38.030
like I couldn't
run more than, say,

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:39.920
one pod of a particular thing.

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:42.240
Is that limitation still
in place with Minikube,

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:44.330
or am I just kind of
resource controlled

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:47.060
based on how big my VM is
more than anything else?

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.040
CARTER MORGAN: You're
going to the node now,

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.270
so you have one node.

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:50.971
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:21:50.971 --> 00:21:53.470
So I can run multiples of those
containers within that node,

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:55.210
and very similar to what
I would run at scale,

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:56.363
but maybe just slightly smaller.

00:21:56.363 --> 00:21:57.279
CARTER MORGAN: Uh-huh.

00:21:57.279 --> 00:21:59.960
And now Minikube is
ongoing, so it's not

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:01.321
like the final finished product.

00:22:01.321 --> 00:22:03.195
Off the top of my head,
I can't remember what

00:22:03.195 --> 00:22:04.790
they're thinking about adding.

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:08.820
But again, you don't get, say,
the load balancers or ingress.

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:12.357
But you can set up
deployments and all the--

00:22:12.357 --> 00:22:13.690
MARK MANDEL: All the good stuff.

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:14.340
CARTER MORGAN: All
the good stuff.

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:15.060
MARK MANDEL: All the good stuff.

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:17.351
But there's more stuff coming,
and they're really like,

00:22:17.351 --> 00:22:20.814
that's the one true way of
doing local development.

00:22:20.814 --> 00:22:22.280
Ooh, ooh, we have
some contention.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.030
CARTER MORGAN: Well,
no, I just-- I always

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:26.695
